{
  "dom_id": "step-q71477",
  "name": "Question 19",
  "problem": "Find the gradient of the product $f(x,y)g(x,y),$ given that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x,y) & =x+y,\\, &  & ∇f(x,y)=\\text{ }i+\\text{ }j, &  &  \\\\ g(x,y) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y),\\, &  & ∇g(x,y)=\\frac{1}{x+y}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{x+y}\\text{ }j. &  & \\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "worked_solution": "The gradient of the product of two functions $f(x,y)$ and $g(x,y)$ is given by:\n\n$$\n∇[fg]=g\\,∇\\,f+f\\,∇\\,g\n$$\n\nSo, using this product rule we obtain: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇[f(x,y)g(x,y)] & =g(x,y)∇f(x,y)+f(x,y)∇g(x,y) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)(\\text{ }i+\\text{ }j)+(x+y)(\\frac{1}{x+y}\\text{ }i+\\frac{1}{x+y}\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)\\text{ }i+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)\\text{ }j+\\frac{x+y}{x+y}\\text{ }i+\\frac{x+y}{x+y}\\text{ }j \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)\\text{ }i+\\text{ }i)+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)\\text{ }j+1\\text{ }j) \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+1)\\text{ }i+(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+y)+1)\\text{ }j\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
